Title: Judge's Crossword Puzzle No. 67
Author: W. T. Litson
Date: 1928-09-01
Byline: Submitted by W. T. Litson, Jr., Oceanside, L. I.
Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-09-01_95/page/n31/mode/1up
Puzzle-Number: 67
Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-09-08_95/page/n32/mode/1up


TRAM#SOS#SHAD
AERO#PEP#PACE
HAIL#ARA#ANNA
GRATER#DANGER
####METER####
PATES#I#MONEY
IDEA#ADO#NONE
TEARS#E#METES
####ASSET####
ESCAPE#ASSERT
YEAR#DIG#CREE
ERIN#ATE#AIDS
RENO#NOR#REST


A1. This helps an Englishman to get along. ~ TRAM
A5. A help wanted signal. ~ SOS
A8. The word naming this fish is two-thirds of the word naming its reflection. ~ SHAD
A12. An abbreviation for a word which is the preoccupation of certain high-minded young men. ~ AERO
A13. The desire for this has led to a lot of monkeygland business. ~ PEP
A14. This is one step. ~ PACE
A15. This should be driven home. ~ HAIL
A16. This is either a macaw or a constellation. (Take your choice.) ~ ARA
A17. This girl's name starts and ends with an article. ~ ANNA
A18. This is rough on the surface. ~ GRATER
A20. A word the wise will heed. ~ DANGER
A22. This is too fast in a taxicab. ~ METER
A24. These shine in the theatre—down front. ~ PATES
A28. [missing] ~ MONEY
A33. This will stay with you if you're tight. ~ IDEA
A34. It takes brains to get this. ~ ADO
A35. Shakespeare made much of this—about nothing. ~ NONE
A36. How many bootleggers favor repeal of the 18th Amendment? ~ TEARS
A38. It's very sad to drop these. ~ METES
A39. What one does who allots, portions out. ~ ASSET
A42. An item that looks well on the balance sheet. ~ ESCAPE
A46. Bars often make this impossible. ~ ASSERT
A51. To insist upon one's rights. ~ YEAR
A52. This will certainly age a person. ~ DIG
A54. What many do to get the dirt. ~ CREE
A55. This is an Indian tribe ~ ERIN
A56. This is ever green. ~ ATE
A57. What Adam did to get his walking-papers. ~ AIDS
A58. These are helpful to military men. ~ RENO
A59. A very famous Western city. ~ NOR
A60. A conjunction. ~ REST

D1. Strong taste or flavor. ~ TAHG
D2. What we'd prefer to bring up in a military Invasion. ~ REAR
D3. This has an air about it. ~ ARIA
D4. To shed. ~ MOLT
D5. What you need to retire. ~ SPARE
D6. This is over for a poet. ~ OER
D7. This gets many a laborer into a hole. ~ SPADE
D8. To extend across. ~ SPAN
D9. What the old desperado did when he got to the end of his rope. ~ HANG
D10. A skin disease. ~ ACNE
D11. A term of endearment. ~ DEAR
D19. Printer's measures. ~ EMS
D21. What the boys like best about the girls. ~ ARM
D23. These are always rising and falling. ~ TIDES
D24. The heart of a peach. ~ PIT
D25. This is soft stuff. (Suffix.) ~ ADE
D26. You get this with your 5 o'clock girl. ~ TEA
D27. This picks up scandal. ~ EAR
D29. [missing] ~ ONE
D30. This is singular. ~ NOT
D31. This is negative. ~ ENE
D32. This is about two o'clock on the compass. ~ YES
D37. An easy word to say in a speakeasy. ~ SAP
D38. This is vital stuff. ~ MTS
D40. These are the high places. (Abbr.) ~ SEDAN
D41. This travels around. ~ EAGER
D42. Full of keen de ire. ~ EYER
D43. What a corner lounger might be called. ~ SERE
D44. Withered; dried up. ~ CAIN
D45. A famous homicide. ~ ARNO
D47. A river of Tuscany. ~ SCAR
D48. A sign of an old wound. ~ ERIE
D49. A lake. ~ REDS
D50. The hobgoblin of some 100% Americans. ~ TEST
D53. This should be applied to prohibition liquor. 54. A foreign statesman made famous by crossword puzzles. ~ ITO


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A15: This should be driven home. → NAIL (answer key prints HAIL; 1 Down 'Strong taste or flavor' is TANG)
D42: Full of keen de ire. → desire

